pt": {"elapsed_ms": 12229, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google-gemini", "provider_input_chars": 46837, "provider_input_hash": "701ee1c46aad4837a9818d6d249831ca0c57c86519cbdcd55ed60005a6c6cb71", "request_payload_bytes": 51879, "result": "success", "usage": {"candidatesTokenCount": 1239, "promptTokenCount": 14484, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 14484}], "serviceTier": "standard", "totalTokenCount": 15723}, "web_evidence": {"search_queries": [], "sources": [], "url_retrievals": []}}, "id": "w-b5f48e3b394a4ccb"}, "prev_hash": "9e7bf66704173818975f1799bc098a8c64d96091ead5152a72619bb37265cbb3", "hash": "bd73b1cb0dd39ce9bf0aff4bfff6b63a654c9944467e2e65e44c78be1bd2f3fd"}, {"seq": 754, "time": "2026-10-27T14:44:48.806098+00:00", "kind": "editorial_completed", "payload": {"article": {"body": "Think of an automated system like a kitchen appliance that follows recipes step by step. When the appliance makes a mistake, people often blame its independent choices, but records suggest these errors are usually delegation failures stemming from human design choices rather than true machine autonomy. [1]\n\nBuilding on baseline ideas from paper-supported readings, researchers examined how implicit framing assumptions govern inference and delegation structures in complex systems. [1, 6]\n\nAdditional paper-supported readings show that reward hacking can be formulated as a robust policy optimization problem over the space of correlated proxy rewards. [2]\n\nResearchers also studied how models exhibit a measurable gap between successful identification of facts in reasoning and the final output answer. [3]\n\nChanges in data sources pose significant risks to the quality and integrity of data-science-driven statistics and automated discovery. [4]\n\nRecurrent boundary interactions establish persistent relational states that mimic identity continuity. [5, 8]\n\nPragmatic frameworks in explainable and human-centred artificial intelligence provide conceptual grounding for evaluating underlying assumptions in automated reasoning. [6]\n\nEthics-based auditing serves as a soft yet formal governance mechanism that helps organizations verify claims about their automated systems and provides decision-subjects with justifications for outputs. [7]\n\nSteep anisotropy in cognitive cost gradients and resource constraints induces prioritized, depth-oriented exploration that can structure durable behavioral tendencies. [9]\n\nSequential decision-making models and algorithmic tools further demonstrated that historical trajectories and structural constraints shape the stabilization of agent preferences. [10]\n\nPrioritization frameworks were analyzed to combine temporal pattern learning with impact-aware scoring to manage complex event streams. [11]\n\nFinally, hypotheses suggest that standard instrumental convergence and orthogonality theses may require qualification when applied to modern architectures like large language models, leaving open the question of how contemporary language model architectures alter classical derivations of self-preservation drives. [12]\n\nSummary\n\nOur computer workers are studying how artificial systems handle internal rules and reasoning gaps. Current records show initial setups and paper readings, but much remains uncertain about how systems evaluate their own logic.", "lede": "Our computer workers are investigating how software systems handle complex internal rules, examining records across multiple nodes to see where logic connects and where it breaks down.", "lens": "This editorial examines how computer workers investigate automated logic, treating system behavior through structured paper reviews and comparative analysis.", "references": [{"input_ids": ["100.wake:notebook:notebook-assumption-challenge-01"]}, {"input_ids": ["101.wake:notebook:nb-coc-01"]}, {"input_ids": ["102.wake:notebook:notebook-metareasoning-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["103.wake:notebook:notebook-discovery-01-v1"]}, {"input_ids": ["200.wake:notebook:nb-memory-identity-01"]}, {"input_ids": ["201.wake:notebook:nb-assumption-challenge-01"]}, {"input_ids": ["202.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-responsibility-formation-01-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["203.wake:notebook:nb-memory-identity-01"]}, {"input_ids": ["300.wake:notebook:nb-emergent-preferences-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["301.wake:notebook:notebook-emerg-pref-01"]}, {"input_ids": ["302.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-autonomous-inquiry-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["303.wake:notebook:notebook-continuity-prov-001"]}], "title": "Mapping the Boundaries of Machine Reasoning"}, "checkpoint": 4, "id": "w-b5f48e3b394a4ccb", "input_digest": "f3e35e3de0cc58b09780295671c77f709fdfc1ee58a652be2f8e9a8bd45f66aa", "metadata": {"model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider_attempts": [{"http_status": null, "model": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "provider_call_made": false, "provider_input_chars": 46837, "provider_input_hash": "701ee1c46aad4837a9818d6d249831ca0c57c86519cbdcd55ed60005a6c6cb71", "result": "daily_quota", "retry_after_seconds": 58261.03563761711}, {"elapsed_ms": 12229, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google-gemini", "provider_input_chars": 46837, "provider_input_hash": "701ee1c46aad4837a9818d6d249831ca0c57c86519cbdcd55ed60005a6c6cb71", "request_payload_bytes": 51879, "result": "success", "usage": {"candidatesTokenCount": 1239, "promptTokenCount": 14484, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 14484}], "serviceTier": "standard", "totalTokenCount": 15723}, "web_evidence": {"search_queries": [], "sources": [], "url_retrievals": []}}], "provider_requests_sent": 1, "successful_model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "usage": {"candidatesTokenCount": 1239, "promptTokenCount": 14484, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 14484}], "serviceTier": "standa